REGISTRY.satellite -- every 8-bit code satellite uses, and what it means.

CHARACTERS AND TOKENS ARE TWO ALPHABETS (the author, 2026-09-16).

THE CHARACTERS are codes 0-127 below, and a string is the same at every width:
an 8-BIT STRING HOLDS ASCII ONLY (the author: "8-bit will be ascii only then"),
a 16-bit string holds those and every character up to U+FFFF, and a 32-bit string
holds everything -- each character its own Unicode number above 127. So the
widths nest: widening a string zero-extends each code, nothing is renumbered,
and two strings of different widths compare code against code.

THE TOKENS are the stored program's alphabet, never a character and never inside
a string's value. A string literal's characters travel in the program behind
string_token, and characters that do not fit behind wide_run.

NEXT (the author, 2026-09-16): the tokens move to 16 bits, so that there is room
for every token the language will ever need instead of the 70 free codes left
here, and this registry becomes that list.

THE HIGH BIT SAYS WHICH: 0xxxxxxx is a character, 1xxxxxxx is a token. One bit
test, everywhere, forever. 00000000 is nothing, as the author chose, so a
zeroed or half-written buffer reads as nothing rather than as a row of a's.
11111111 is kept back: it says the next byte carries the token, which is how
this list grows past 256 without any of it being rewritten.

The order of the characters is the author's (2026-09-16), and comparing codes
sorts text in that order: a before z before A before 0 before ! before space.

ONE FILE, ONE ANSWER. The lexer, the interpreter and the .satc writer all read
this registry, so none of them can drift about what a code means. Like
003's words.def, it is exempt from the 300-line rule.

